    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Reverse Mortgage vs Selling and Downsizing: How to Think Through the Real Decision</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you're 62 or older and your home represents a significant portion of your net worth, you've probably already considered two options: tap the equity through a reverse mortgage and stay put, or sell the home, downsize, and free up the capital that way. Both paths have real merit. Neither is obviously right for everyone. What most people lack is a framework for comparing them honestly, and that's what this article provides.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Question Behind the Question</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The choice between a reverse mortgage and selling isn't really about the product. It's about what you want the next chapter of your life to look like and what role your home plays in that picture. Staying in the home you've lived in for decades has value that doesn't show up in a financial model. So does the freedom and simplicity that can come with a smaller, lower-maintenance property. Getting the financial comparison right matters, but so does being honest about the non-financial factors before the numbers take over the conversation.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Hidden Tradeoff Between Liquidity and Stability</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's the fundamental difference between these two paths. Selling and downsizing gives you liquidity. You convert your equity to cash, reduce your housing costs, and potentially eliminate a mortgage entirely. The money is in your hands, deployable however you choose. The tradeoff is disruption: moving costs, real estate transaction costs, the process of finding and purchasing a new home, and the emotional weight of leaving a place that may hold significant personal history.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A reverse mortgage gives you stability. You stay in your home, access a portion of your equity without selling, and preserve the continuity of your daily life. The tradeoff is that the equity you access comes with accumulating interest, and the total cost of that access over time is higher than the cost of simply selling.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Neither path is free. The question is which costs you're more willing to accept.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How the Financial Comparison Actually Works</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Selling and downsizing generates a net proceed: your sale price minus agent commissions, closing costs, and any remaining mortgage balance. If you purchase a less expensive home, the difference between your net proceeds and the new purchase price becomes accessible capital. If you rent instead of repurchasing, you free up more capital but introduce ongoing housing costs with no equity building.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A reverse mortgage generates accessible equity without a transaction, but the loan balance grows over time as interest accrues. The longer you remain in the home, the larger the balance and the smaller the equity remaining. If home values appreciate during that period, appreciation offsets some of the balance growth. If they don't, the equity cushion erodes more quickly.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The comparison also includes the costs you avoid by staying. No moving costs. No real estate commissions on a sale. No purchase transaction costs on a new home. Those avoided costs belong in the analysis.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Get a realistic estimate of your home's current market value before you compare anything.</strong> Both paths depend on that number. An informal estimate from a real estate agent or a formal appraisal gives you the foundation for an honest comparison.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculate your net proceeds from a sale after commissions, closing costs, and any remaining mortgage.</strong> That's what selling actually puts in your hands, not the sale price.</li>
      <li><strong>Identify what a realistic downsizing target looks like in your market.</strong> What would you buy or rent, and what would it cost? The gap between your net proceeds and that cost is your freed capital under the selling scenario.</li>
      <li><strong>Request a reverse mortgage estimate based on your age, home value, and equity position.</strong> That shows you what you could access, how the balance grows over time, and what equity would remain at various points in the future.</li>
      <li><strong>Put both scenarios side by side with the same time horizon.</strong> Compare where your financial position looks at five, ten, and fifteen years under each path. Include housing costs, available capital, and remaining equity or net worth in the comparison.</li>
      <li><strong>Give the non-financial factors their weight.</strong> How important is staying in your current home? How do you feel about the process of moving? Those answers belong in the decision alongside the numbers.</li>
    </ol>

